      <div style="font-size:0.8rem;color:#fca5a5;font-weight:700;">General Conference Corporation of Seventh-day Adventists — April 15, 1904 — Washington, D.C.</div>
      <a href="/april-15-1904-new-organization#signers" class="hnb hnb-gold" style="margin-top:18px;display:inline-block;">Meet the 5 Signers &rarr;</a>
    </div>
    <div style="background:rgba(212,165,55,0.07);border-left:4px solid #d4a537;border-radius:0 14px 14px 0;padding:22px 26px;margin-bottom:24px;">
      <p style="font-size:1.07rem;color:rgba(254,243,199,0.93);line-height:1.85;margin:0 0 14px;">
        The 1850 Prophetic Chart, created under divine direction, warned that the final apostasy would be the formation of an <strong style="color:#fcd34d;">Image of the Papacy</strong> — a Protestant church uniting with the state, copying Rome's structure, and enforcing religious authority through civil law. In 1904, under President <a href="/april-15-1904-new-organization#signer-daniells" style="color:#fcd34d;text-decoration:underline;">Arthur G. Daniells</a>, and in open defiance of the counsel of Ellen G. White, the General Conference leadership incorporated the church in Washington, D.C. as a civil corporation. The April 15, 1904 articles list the five signers who executed it: <a href="/pioneers/james-r-scott" style="color:#fcd34d;text-decoration:underline;">James R. Scott</a>, <a href="/pioneers/harvey-edson-rogers" style="color:#fcd34d;text-decoration:underline;">Harvey Edson Rogers</a>, <a href="/pioneers/daniel-k-nicola" style="color:#fcd34d;text-decoration:underline;">Daniel K. Nicola</a>, <a href="/april-15-1904-new-organization#signer-daniells" style="color:#fcd34d;text-decoration:underline;">Arthur G. Daniells</a>, and <a href="/pioneers/amos-p-needham" style="color:#fcd34d;text-decoration:underline;">Amos P. Needham</a>. <a href="/april-15-1904-new-organization#signers" style="color:#fcd34d;text-decoration:underline;">Meet the 5 Signers &rarr;</a></p>
        <p style="font-size:1.02rem;color:rgba(254,243,199,0.88);line-height:1.8;margin:0;">
          The "Image of the Papacy" is not a statue — it is a system: a church that wields state power, enforces its will by law, and repeats the union of church and state that marked the Dark Ages. The 1850 chart mapped the danger; the 1904 corporation made it reality. The prophecy is fulfilled — and the warning stands for all who will see.
        </p>
